From 6b15594b1e4dd650b0a398182f11625408a90f9d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yvan Janssens <yvanj@cyber.gent>
Date: Thu, 26 Nov 2026 13:50:35 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 1/3] KVM: s390: Let user space inject external interruptions
 it emulates

Some external interruptions are defined by a hypervisor, not by the
machine: z/VM presents those of its inter-user communication vehicle
(IUCV) with code 0x4000, enabled by bit 62 of control register 0. A VMM
that provides such a service in user space cannot present the
interruption, because each injection type of KVM_S390_IRQ stands for one
interruption that KVM itself knows.

Add the type KVM_S390_INT_EXT_USER, an external interruption described
by user space: its code, the control register 0 bits that enable it and,
optionally, a CPU address and parameters to store. A vCPU holds at most
one. KVM presents it like the other external interruptions, with the
lowest priority among them, in the format of the architectural mode of
the vCPU, and includes it in KVM_S390_GET_IRQ_STATE and
KVM_S390_SET_IRQ_STATE.

The capability KVM_CAP_S390_USER_EXT_IRQ makes the type available; its
argument holds the control register 0 bits user space may use.

